-- title:
--   Lemma 2 (Desai et al. 2012a, Thm 4), p. 61 — a supersolution V̂ bounds its penalty's inner problem: W^ẑ(T, y) ≤ V̂(T, y)
-- statement:
--   The model is the Bayesian bandit of §2.1: arms $a \in \mathcal A = \{1,\dots,K\}$ (0-based in Lean, $K \ge 1$), reward laws $\mathcal R_a(\theta_a)$, conjugate priors $\mathcal P_a(y_a)$, the Bayesian update $\mathcal U_a$, and the outcome law $\mathcal I(y)$ under which the arms are independent, $\theta_a \sim \mathcal P_a(y_a)$, and the rewards of arm $a$ are i.i.d. $\mathcal R_a(\theta_a)$ given $\theta_a$. The standing assumptions of §2.1 hold: $\mathcal U_a(y_a,r)$ is the Bayes posterior after observing $r$, the update maps are measurable, and $\mathbb E_{y_a}|R_{a,1}| < \infty$ for every belief $y_a$ (3).
--
--   Let $\hat V : \mathbb N_0 \times \mathcal Y \to \mathbb R$ be a supersolution (Definition 2) and $\hat z_t$ the penalty (168) generated by $\hat V$. Then for every outcome $\omega$ the maximal value of the inner problem,
--
--   $$V_1^{\hat z,in}(\emptyset,\omega;T,y) = \max_{a_{1:T}\in\mathcal A^T} \sum_{t=1}^T \big(r_t(a_{1:t},\omega) - \hat z_t(a_{1:t},\omega;T,y)\big),$$
--
--   is at most $\hat V(T,y)$; consequently
--
--   $$W^{\hat z}(T,y) \le \hat V(T,y). \tag{169}$$
--
--   A supersolution of the Bellman equation is therefore an upper bound on the penalised clairvoyant value, outcome by outcome.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The payoff $r_t - \hat z_t$ uses the conjugate closed forms of the conditional expectations given $H_{t-1}$ (see the Setting). The integral form (169) is stated under the added, disclosed hypothesis that $\omega \mapsto V_1^{\hat z,in}(\emptyset,\omega;T,y)$ is integrable, since for a general $\hat V$ the expectation need not exist; the pathwise bound needs no such hypothesis.
-- source:
--   Min, Maglaras, Moallemi, Thompson Sampling with Information Relaxation Penalties, arXiv:1902.04251v2, p. 61, Lemma 2, (168)–(169)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_InfoRelaxTS_Monotone_Setting

namespace InfoRelaxTS.Monotone

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

/-- Lemma 2 (Theorem 4 of Desai et al. (2012a), rephrased), p. 61, (169): if `V̂` is a supersolution,
then for every outcome `ω` the maximal value of the inner problem under the penalty generated by `V̂`
is at most `V̂(T, y)` (the stronger sense); hence, when that maximal value is integrable,
`W^ẑ(T, y) ≤ V̂(T, y)`. -/
theorem lemma_2 {K : ℕ} [NeZero K] {Θ Y : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Θ] [StandardBorelSpace Θ]
    [Nonempty Θ] [MeasurableSpace Y]
    (R : Fin K → Kernel Θ ℝ) [∀ a, IsMarkovKernel (R a)]
    (P : Fin K → Kernel Y Θ) [∀ a, IsMarkovKernel (P a)]
    (U : Fin K → Y → ℝ → Y) (hM : StandingAssumptions R P U)
    (Vhat : ℕ → (Fin K → Y) → ℝ) (hV : IsSupersolution R P U Vhat) (T : ℕ) (y : Fin K → Y) :
    (∀ ω, innerMax R P U y Vhat T ω ≤ Vhat T y) ∧
      (Integrable (innerMax R P U y Vhat T) (I R P y) → penBound R P U Vhat T y ≤ Vhat T y) := by sorry

end InfoRelaxTS.Monotone
